// Editable component-output table (v0.663) -- closes gap #4 in
// docs/design/homestead-solo-design.md section 7 and implements the "editable
// component-output table (infinite-of-X data)" that docs/design/self-sufficiency.md's
// "What data we'd add" section calls for. One entry per generation / collection / recycling
// machine in data/machines/home.ron's catalog: an output figure + its assumptions, so the
// self-sufficiency score is COMPUTED from a queryable data file instead of parsed out of the
// hand-typed stat strings baked into home.ron. Every `id` here MUST be a machine in the
// home.ron catalog (enforced by a unit test). Loaded by src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs.
// Data-driven (infinite-of-X): tune a number or add a machine here, no code change.
//
// FIELDS:
//   id           - a machine catalog id in data/machines/home.ron (and home_solo.ron).
//   output_value - the machine's per-unit output in `unit`.
//   unit         - the output's unit. NOTE the unit is HETEROGENEOUS across rows (kWh/day for
//                  energy generation, L/day for water collection, kg/day for nutrient
//                  recycling, kW for a runtime-gated backup, kg/day-CO2 and m3/h-air for life support). The
//                  loader's household_energy_balance() sums only the "kWh/day" rows for the
//                  energy score; a water/nutrient/air score sums its own unit the same way.
//   assumptions  - the editable, human-readable derivation the operator can tune.
//
// AGREEMENT with home.ron (2026-09-27): the generation rows below are the catalog's own figures
// at the home's site, the same numbers the Construction page's power meter credits
// (data/machines/home.ron quotes every source beside the machine): the solar panel's PVWatts
// yield at location.ron's Silverdale, WA, less a battery round trip, and the wind turbine's
// average in the site's measured wind. `household_energy_supply_sums_kwh_per_day_components` in
// src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs holds them to the catalog.
//
// HISTORY: until 2026-09-27 the panel read 1.44 kWh/day (400 W x a generic 4.5 sun-hours x
// 0.80, no place attached) and the turbine 0.7 (an unsourced 19% capacity factor). The
// 2026-07-30 life-support balance pass had already found the panel disagreeing with
// location.ron (3.9 sun-hours a year's average and about 1.3 in December at Silverdale, 1.17
// kWh/day with a 0.75 derate), and a pinned test (4 panels = 5.76 +/- 0.5) kept it there.
// PVWatts' own answer for a 40 degree tilt, 1.09 after the batteries, sits between them.
(
    components: [
        // ── Energy GENERATION (kWh/day, so household_energy_balance() sums these) ──
        (
            id: "solar_panel", output_value: 1.09, unit: "kWh/day",
            assumptions: "One 400 W panel at location.ron's Silverdale, WA: NREL PVWatts 8.5.0 (queried 2026-09-27; fixed open rack, 40 degree tilt facing south, the default 14.08% system losses, NSRDB PSM V3 tmy-2020 weather) gives 440.68 kWh a year at the inverter, 3.906 kWh/m2/day on the panel and a 12.58% capacity factor; x 0.90 for the battery round trip (Tesla Powerwall 2 datasheet, 'Round Trip Efficiency 90%'), taken on all of it = 45.3 W averaged, 1.09 kWh/day a year's average and 0.51 in January, the worst month. It read 1.44 (a generic 4.5 sun-hours x 0.80, no place attached).",
        ),
        (
            id: "wind_turbine_small", output_value: 0.11, unit: "kWh/day",
            assumptions: "Primus Wind Power AIR 40 ('Rated Power: 160 watts @ 28 mph (12.5 m/s)', start up 3.13 m/s, '40 kWh/month @ 12 mph (5.5 m/s)', its owner's manual) in the site's wind: the Bremerton airport averages 5.3 mph, 2.37 m/s (Western Regional Climate Center, 1996-2006). Its power as a cube law from its start-up speed, capped at 160 W and fitted to the manual's 40 kWh a month, over a Rayleigh distribution (how AWEA 9.1-2009 rates small turbines) = 4.4 W averaged, 0.11 kWh/day, a 2.8% capacity factor (range 3.7 to 4.7 W). It read 0.7, an unsourced 19% capacity factor; PNNL's 2024 Distributed Wind Market Report finds 13% for small wind projects sited where the wind is.",
        ),
        // ── Backup generation (rated power, NOT a daily energy -- unit differs on purpose;
        //    excluded from the daily kWh/day supply sum until runtime hours are modeled) ──
        (
            id: "generator_portable", output_value: 1.8, unit: "kW",
            assumptions: "A Honda EU2200i class inverter generator: 'Rated output' 1.8 kVA and a 3.6 L tank that lasts '3.2hr @ rated load' (its owner's manual and a Honda dealer's listing, read 2026-09-27), so 1.8 kWh for 1.125 L of fuel each hour it runs. Rated power, not a daily figure: what it makes depends on the hours it is run, so it is NOT summed into the passive kWh/day supply, and the power meter shows it apart. It read 2 kW. Covers the deep no-sun tail.",
        ),
        // ── Water COLLECTION / treatment (L/day) ──
        (
            id: "water_tank", output_value: 144.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "Passive rain catchment at 0.1 L/min (home.ron port flow) = 144 L/day, no power. Also stores 8000 L (the dry-season buffer).",
        ),
        (
            id: "water_pump", output_value: 2880.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "Powered well lift at 2.0 L/min (home.ron port) = 2880 L/day WHEN RUN. Power-gated: cutting the grid stops the inflow. Runs a fraction of the day in practice.",
        ),
        (
            id: "water_purifier", output_value: 240.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "+240 L/day potable throughput (home.ron stat). Polishes water the pump lifted; not a net source, so it is a treatment cap, not new supply.",
        ),
        // ── Nutrient / waste RECYCLING (kg/day nutrient; also +greywater) ──
        (
            id: "composter", output_value: 1.2, unit: "kg/day",
            assumptions: "+1.2 kg/day of FINISHED COMPOST (not of nutrient: the actual N+P+K in 1.2 kg of compost is about 18 g N, 6 g P, 12 g K, roughly 70x smaller) from food + crop + human waste, plus greywater back to irrigation (home.ron stats). NOTE the scale: home.ron is the 3-PERSON home, so its +155 L/day greywater goes with a 255 L/day household (three of the one-person fixture list, since 2026-09-27), a plausible 61% recovery. For ONE person the household is 85 L/day and greywater is about 59 L/day; the 155 figure must never be copied into a solo design, where it would imply 194% recovery. One person makes about 4.3 kg/day of wet organic material once crop residue is counted, not 1.5 kg/day (that figure is excreta only).",
        ),
        // ── Air / life support (2026-09-26: ship life support, systems::life_support) ──
        (
            id: "air_recycler", output_value: 4.74, unit: "kg/day-CO2",
            assumptions: "Now the CO2 scrubber: the ISS Carbon Dioxide Removal Assembly, a four-bed molecular sieve that vents what it catches overboard. 4.74 kg of CO2 a day at a 267 Pa (2.0 torr) inlet (Peters and Knox, ICES-2017-241), 860 W operational average and 1,487 W peak (Hanford 2004, NASA/CR-2004-208941 Table 7.3.2). It runs only above 2,636 ppm (BVAD 2022's coming 0.267 kPa crew limit) and removes proportionally less in thinner air. It no longer makes oxygen: the 25 W air recycler this entry described until 2026-09-26 was an air throughput figure, and aboard only the crops make oxygen (data/life_support.ron). In both shipped homes the crops take up the carbon dioxide and the scrubber idles.",
        ),
        (
            id: "air_handler", output_value: 1842.0, unit: "m3/h-air",
            assumptions: "A ducted chilled-water fan coil (Carrier 42CT size 14: 1,084 CFM at 50 Pa on 325 W of power input) on the station's cooling loop, its air leaving at the ISS condensing heat exchanger's 5.9 C dew point. What it returns to the tanks is not a fixed figure: it condenses the air it moves down to that dew point, so each litre costs about 27 Wh of fan power in a greenhouse held at 75% and about 105 Wh in the home's own air held at 50% (docs/design/ship-life-support.md).",
        ),
    ],
)
